        reworked: `<ul><li>A reworked piece is one you made by changing the design of one pre-owned piece made by another brand, for example by cutting it into a new shape, resewing it, dyeing it, or adding your own material.</li><li>A repair or an alteration that keeps the original design, such as hemming, taking in, replacing a button, or fixing a seam, doesn’t make a piece reworked. List that piece as pre-owned, and name the repair or alteration in the condition notes.</li><li>Required: check “Reworked from another brand’s pre-owned piece,” and fill in Original brand and Original item. Panopie then shows this sentence at the top of the listing: “Reworked by [your store] from a pre-owned [brand] [item]. Not made or endorsed by [brand].”</li><li>Required: set Condition to Pre-owned and choose a grade. The grade describes the wear on the original piece, not your new work.</li><li>Required: in the condition notes, say what you changed and describe the wear on the original piece. For example: “Cut down from a jacket into a vest, with a new hem and new armholes. Light fading on the back of the original jacket.”</li><li>Required: the Brand field is your own label, or your store’s name if you don’t use a label. It can’t be the brand of the original piece.</li><li>Required: your label or store name is sewn, printed, or heat-pressed inside the piece. A hang tag alone isn’t enough. When you send the piece for review, check “Our label or store name is sewn, printed, or heat-pressed inside this piece.” We don’t ask for a photo of it, and we don’t check it when we review the listing. If someone reports the listing, we may ask you about the label, and we may take the listing down or send it back to you while we check. Your label doesn’t replace any label the law requires.</li><li>Leave the original maker’s labels where they are. Don’t cut them out.</li><li>Don’t put the original brand in the title. Leave Series / model empty: Original item already says what the piece was made from. You may name the original brand in the description or condition notes, but only to say what the piece was made from. Don’t say or suggest that the original brand made, sponsored, or approved your piece, or that it works with you.</li><li>A reworked piece can’t use the Vintage style or the tags “archive” or “retro,” and it has no vintage type or era. Don’t use “vintage,” “deadstock,” “new old stock,” “archive,” or “retro” in the title or description. If you know how old the original piece is, you may say so in the condition notes, as your own statement.</li><li>If the original piece had no brand or label, don’t check Reworked. List it under your own label as pre-owned, and say what you changed in the condition notes.</li><li>In Material or fabric content, write “reused” before each fiber of the original piece, for example “80% reused cotton, 20% reused polyester”, and add the fiber content of anything you added. If you don’t know the fiber content of the original piece, write “Secondhand materials, fiber content not known” and choose Other as the main material. You are responsible for any label the law requires on a piece you sell.</li><li>Not accepted: adding another brand’s logo, label, patch, or hardware; pieces made from a brand’s new fabric or unused stock; and pieces sewn together from more than one brand’s pieces.</li></ul>`,
